  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "If the point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),$ then its Cartesian coordinates are\n\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{3},-2).$",
